WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.374
Common random numbers give each candidate
parameter the same underlying shocks.

2
00:00:05.374 --> 00:00:09.028
Keep this simulated world fixed while
comparing candidates.

3
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.161
Fixed shocks do not mean fixed outcomes.

4
00:00:15.161 --> 00:00:17.169
Change productivity dispersion, and those

5
00:00:17.169 --> 00:00:21.586
same shocks produce different simulated
firms and a different model moment.

6
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:29.449
If every candidate instead gets fresh
shocks, its moment changes for two

7
00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:32.424
reasons: the parameter and the simulated
world.

8
00:00:32.424 --> 00:00:34.974
Reusing shocks removes that extra change.

9
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:41.523
Conditional on the fixed draws, we can
compare parameter changes more clearly.

10
00:00:41.523 --> 00:00:42.816
Simulation error remains;

11
00:00:42.816 --> 00:00:47.558
averaging more simulated data sets
addresses that separate source of error.
